  m_Name: Module8
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PhotoTextContentData
  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: d33aa96233e8d0d41a38360dfc31153c, type: 3}
    titleName: 'BIOS / UIEF CONFIGURATION

'
    description: "Introduction\r\n \r\nThe BIOS (pronounced as \"bay-os\") or the
      basic input/output system of the computer, is responsible in making sure that
      all the other chips, hard drives, ports, and the CPU function together.\r\nThe
      BIOS basically sets up your computer -- it loads your drivers and boots the
      operating system.\r\n\n\nThe BIOS is a software usually built onto the motherboard
      that shows the major hardware components of your computer with the operating
      system.\r\n \r\nIt is usually stored on a Flash memory chip on the motherboard,
      but sometimes the chip is another type of ROM.\r\n \r\nWhen you turn on your
      computer, the BIOS does several things. The sequence is as follows:\r\n \r\n1. 
      Check the CMOS Setup for custom settings\r\n2.  Load the interrupt handlers
      and device drivers\r\n3.  Initialize registers and power management\r\n4. 
      Perform the power-on self-test (POST)\r\n5.  Display system settings\r\n6. 
      Determine which devices are bootable\r\n7.   Initiate the bootstrap sequence\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 884747b1ad71bf44b8ffdc84af7857c3, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: 'Configuring the BIOS

      By default, the first boot of the
      newly installed computer is on the hard disk--may it be on a laptop or a desktop
      computer. When installing an operating system, the BIOS should be configured
      to set the system to boot on the operating system to be installed. It should
      boot first on the USB installer.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9f08b107c8ceff04ead0bc7529ae36a7, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: 'Running the BIOS

      Upon turning the computer on, press the
      keys that would run Setup. On most computers, it would be keys: ESC, DEL, F1,
      F2, F12, Ctrl-Esc, or Ctrl-Alt-Esc to enter setup. There is usually a line
      of text at the bottom of the display that tells you "Press ___ to Enter Setup."

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: e6c91a80d502a4a4bb640348cc21a9d7, type: 3}
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 1487c9d411d30e8489f0c243b70497b0, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: 'BIOS setup would depend on the motherboard manufacturer. However,
      these are the common, standard options you may see in the BIOS setup:

      1.   
      If you would like to boot from a certain device, say a USB Flash Drive to install
      an Operating System (which we will demonstrate later), click advanced set-up
      and look for the first, second, and third boot device.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: a4088dd65ac3fec43afc91aa89e7eb11, type: 3}
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: d4a3c32160073dc408ed4602849bb7c3, type: 3}
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 15d54609b35f0a441b208dc4484c765f, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: 2.    Select first boot device. By default, it would be the hard
      drive on the installed computer.
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 4af000f598742c94499bb73c89bf07f8, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "3.    Make sure to choose \"Save Changes\" when you are changing
      settings in the BIOS. Afterwards, this will restart the computer so the new
      settings take effect.\n\n\n\xB7         System Time/Date - Set the system time
      and date\r\n\xB7         Boot Sequence - The order that BIOS will try to load
      the operating system\r\n\xB7         Plug and Play - A standard for auto-detecting
      connected devices; should be set to \"Yes\" if your computer and operating
      system both support it\r\n\xB7         Mouse/Keyboard - \"Enable Num Lock,\"
      \"Enable the Keyboard,\" \"Auto-Detect Mouse\"...\r\n\xB7         Drive Configuration
      - Configure hard drives, CD-ROM and floppy drives\r\n\xB7         Memory -
      Direct the BIOS to shadow to a specific memory address\r\n\xB7         Security
      - Set a password for accessing the computer\r\n\xB7         Power Management
      - Select whether to use power management, as well as set the amount of time
      for standby and suspend\r\n\xB7         Exit - Save your changes, discard your
      changes or restore default settings\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
